t  the problem is almost everything written  from here onwards while the premise of  the comic is pretty straightforward  hunters hunt things and somehow wind up  being in a seemingly  in the series of battle tournaments and  i have to say this is one of the most  puzzling takeaways that the writer has  when i think of hunter hunter the one  thing that almost never comes to mind is  tournaments and it makes me genuinely  wonder if the author is mistaking hunter  hunter with yuhaka show tagashi's  previous series which is much more  characterized and stereotyped with the  tournament stigma i mean  i guess at a stretch you could say that  hunt hunter has two  tournament style things once during the  hunter exam's final phase and the  short-lived heavens arena but at the  same time neither of those were even  tournaments in the classical sense but  from there nothing else in the series  even comes close to taking on that  format  you know you have the zoldik family york  new city greed island chimera antarc the  chairman election the succession war and  it's all just  nothing nothing to do with tournaments  and all of that together is about 95  percent of the series right there so i'm  already quite perplexed but  let's continue if you've read or watched  hunter hunter and we hope you have  because this article would be a pretty  weird way to get started on it then you  might have noticed something  a little unusual about the way the story  is structured it's essentially a series  of increasingly challenging tournaments  that reveal the strongest people in the  world only to have the next tournament  reveal  someone stronger so i have both read and  watched hunter hunter and i will concur  that this article would be a weird way  to get started on it because the really  unusual thing about the article  is that it completely misrepresents  hunter hunter it starts with the hunter  exam in which only 1  out of every 10 000 applicants is  accepted and even those are winnowed  down by phases that leave only 24  applicants by the time the last one  starts and we just want to pause on this  statement for a second because it is  factually incorrect  by the time the final phase of the  hunter exam began there were only nine  applicants left which i find to be a  really  interesting error to make since the  final phase was a weird tournament which  seems to be the one thing that this  writer pays super close attention to  so i think that's a weird error to make  and it's almost as if the writer maybe  doesn't know what they're talking about  and is confusing the final phase with  the fourth phase  and usually i wouldn't be quite this  petty but at the same time if you're a  paid writer publishing very strong  opinions on a gigantic mega platform  then the very least you should be able  to do is get the facts right  this is the untold truth of hunt hunter  after all and right here we have a  blatant lie of hunter hunter then it  turns out there's a super secret final  part of the exam that happens at the  heavens arena which is full of fighters  so strong that even hunters can't beat  them and here we have some more  delightful misinformation the secret  part  of the exam does indeed exist but it is  not held at heaven's arena gone and  kilauea went to heaven's arena of their  own accord to train at which point gone  encountered wing and began the secret  phase but every other applicant  completed the secret phase in isolated  conditions like karapika who did his  just  randomly in the woods there is no  tournament aspect of the secret phase  and i think there's also a pretty  fundamental misunderstanding of what  hunters are here because almost none of  them participate in heaven's arena in  fact gone and hisoka are the only two to  do so kilauea  technically because he became a hunter  later but if hunters actually did  participate in heaven's arena then they  would wreck  99 of the competitors because that was  one of the key themes of heaven's arena  that these fighters were like the nen  rejects of the world they weren't ready  for it like guido or those who  completely misused it like castro and  all of that is to say once again that  this writer probably didn't pay too much  attention to the series that they feel  so confident about explaining the reason  behind the structure is pretty simple  fighting tournaments have a long and  proud history in shona manga because  they're one of the easiest ways to  introduce new adversaries and get  straight into the big fight scenes  without having to worry about things  like complex plotting or character  motivation  and that's not a criticism and while i  do think that many many fans would  disagree with that last statement sure  let's say it's not a criticism  but that still doesn't make it true and  at this point i just legitimately cannot  comprehend the author's insistence that  hunter hunter is a never-ending series  of tournaments  i did say before that 95 of the series  has nothing to do with the tournament  but that is obvious hyperbole so let's  actually work it out and we will be the  most generous we possibly can be as well  let's say that both the hunter exam and  heaven's arena count as tournament arcs  in their entirety which you can  certainly argue that they don't but  the hunter exam is 38 chapters and  heaven's arena is 20 chapters so  together that's 58 chapters of potential  tournament related content meanwhile  hunter hunter as it stands is 390  chapters long so that 58 chapters of  alleged tournament content  doesn't even make up 15 of the overall  series yet the writer just goes  on and on and on about this never-ending  tournament structure it really is one of  the most baffling things i've ever read  and i can't quite think of a reasonable  explanation for it because other parts  of the article do make it clear that the  writer at least made it to york new city  and even greed island possibly  even further so at this point the writer  does know that what they're saying is  simply factually incorrect but they're  saying it anyway because of  reasons the best explanation i have is  that it would be a condition to activate  the inability which  would make more sense but speaking of i  am very excited to hear their thoughts  on net a power system that can be quite  confusing even when you are paying  attention speaking of nine let's talk  about one of the most notable and  bizarre plot elements that frequently  crops up in the series in its most basic  form nin  is a technique that allows people to tap  into a sort of life force that runs  through all living things  giving those who master its strange  seemingly magical abilities that can  take forms as varied as sensing things  or clouding their adversary's minds or  even shooting out lightning those users  skilled enough to manipulate this force  are called  jedi knights no wait sorry they're just  called nen users got the notes mixed up  there  and i do believe that last part i think  the writer did indeed get their notes  mixed up at all stages of the article  because the only other explanation would  be that they simply did not really watch  or read hunter hunter and not only that  but after completely skimping over the  series they believe that they have the  right to be snarky about it like the  jedi knight comment stuff like that is  kind of infuriating for me to read  because not only is it making it clear  that writer has next to no knowledge on  the subject of none but they're also  making it clear that they don't care to  know either because it's one thing to  make a simple mistake i have made a  whole ton of them on this channel  because hunter hunter is complicated  layered and nuanced especially when it  comes to net  so much more so than you could ever  comprehend on a first reading or viewing  but then again it's a completely  different thing to see killer develop an  electricity-based inability and then  deliberately conflate 